# Clean Code — Tests

Test code is held to the same quality standard as production code. Dirty tests are worse than no tests: they rot, mislead, and are eventually deleted.

## TEST-01 — Fast

Tests must run quickly. Slow tests are skipped, and skipped tests stop providing the safety net they were written to provide.

## TEST-02 — Independent

Each test must set up its own state and clean up after itself. Tests must not depend on execution order. Failing one should never cascade into failing others.

## TEST-03 — Repeatable

A test must produce the same result in every environment — development, CI, offline — without relying on external services, shared databases, or wall-clock time.

## TEST-04 — Self-validating

A test must produce a boolean result: pass or fail. Reading logs or comparing output files manually is not validation. Use assertions.

## TEST-05 — Timely

Write tests alongside — or just before — the production code they cover. Tests written long after the fact are harder to write because the code was not designed with testability in mind.

## TEST-06 — One concept per test

Each test should verify a single logical scenario. Multiple unrelated assertions in one test obscure which behaviour failed and make the test harder to name accurately.

```
// bad — two unrelated concepts in one test
function testUserCreation()
  user = createUser("Alice")
  assert user.name == "Alice"
  deleteUser(user.id)
  assert getUser(user.id) == null  // depends on state from above
end function

// good — each test is independent and focused
function testCreateUser_setsName()
  user = createUser("Alice")
  assert user.name == "Alice"
end function

function testDeleteUser_removesRecord()
  user = fixtures.existingUser()
  deleteUser(user.id)
  assert getUser(user.id) == null
end function
```

## TEST-07 — Readable

Use the Arrange-Act-Assert pattern. Structure tests so the setup, action, and assertion are clearly distinct and easy to follow at a glance.
